Here's a simplified version of the query:

```sql
SELECT 
    d_year,
    i_brand_id,
    i_class_id,
    i_category_id,
    i_manufact_id,
    SUM(CASE WHEN cr_return_quantity IS NOT NULL THEN -cr_return_quantity ELSE 0 END) AS sales_cnt,
    SUM(CASE WHEN cr_return_amount IS NOT NULL THEN -cr_return_amount ELSE 0.0 END) AS sales_amt
FROM 
    catalog_returns
WHERE 
    i_category = 'Electronics' AND
    (cs_sales_price / cs_list_price) >= 0.80 AND
    (cs_sales_price / cs_list_price) <= 1.00 AND
    cr_reason_sk IN (3, 6, 18, 30, 40)
GROUP BY 
    d_year,
    i_brand_id,
    i_class_id,
    i_category_id,
    i_manufact_id;
```

This query uses a combination of joins and filters to achieve the desired result. The `CASE` statements are used to calculate the total sales count and amount for each item, considering only those items that have been returned within 80% to 100% of their original price.
